/T (ABSTRACT)
/E (ABSTRACT)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 264 0 R
/T (Warehouses and fulfilment centres increasingly rely on fleets of autonomous mobile robots to move goods between storage racks and packing docks. The efficiency of such a fleet depends on two coupled problems: finding a short, safe route for each robot through a cluttered, aisle-based floor plan, and ensuring that several robots sharing the same aisles never collide or deadlock. Naive solutions plan each robot independently with a shortest-path algorithm; this produces cost-optimal individual routes but, as this project shows, frequently yields robots that occupy the same cell or swap positions in the same time step.)
/E (Warehouses and fulfilment centres increasingly rely on fleets of autonomous mobile robots to move goods between storage racks and packing docks. The efficiency of such a fleet depends on two coupled problems: finding a short, safe route for each robot through a cluttered, aisle-based floor plan, and ensuring that several robots sharing the same aisles never collide or deadlock. Naive solutions plan each robot independently with a shortest-path algorithm; this produces cost-optimal individual routes but, as this project shows, frequently yields robots that occupy the same cell or swap positions in the same time step.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
