/T (4.5 Admissibility and Optimality of A*)
/E (4.5 Admissibility and Optimality of A*)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 285 0 R
/T (Let C* be the optimal cost from start to goal. Because h is admissible, every node n on an optimal path satisfies f\(n\) = g*\(n\) + h\(n\) <= C*. A* always expands a node with the lowest f value, so when the goal is first removed from the open list its f value equals its g value and no unexpanded node has a lower f; no cheaper path can therefore exist. With a consistent heuristic, f values are non-decreasing along any path and each node is expanded at most once, which is the property exploited by the closed list. This explains why A* returned the same cost as Dijkstra on every benchmark query.)
/E (Let C* be the optimal cost from start to goal. Because h is admissible, every node n on an optimal path satisfies f\(n\) = g*\(n\) + h\(n\) <= C*. A* always expands a node with the lowest f value, so when the goal is first removed from the open list its f value equals its g value and no unexpanded node has a lower f; no cheaper path can therefore exist. With a consistent heuristic, f values are non-decreasing along any path and each node is expanded at most once, which is the property exploited by the closed list. This explains why A* returned the same cost as Dijkstra on every benchmark query.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1010 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 285 0 R
/T (4.6 Multi-Robot Coordination: Space-Time A*)
/E (4.6 Multi-Robot Coordination: Space-Time A*)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1011 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 285 0 R
/T (For a fleet, planning is performed over states \(x, y, t\). From \(x, y, t\) a robot may move to a neighbouring free cell or wait in place, arriving at time t + 1; every action costs one time step. The heuristic is the Manhattan distance to the goal, which remains admissible in the time dimension. Robots are planned in a fixed priority order. After a robot is planned, its path is written to the reservation table \(Section 4.7\). A robot that reaches its goal stays there, so its goal cell is reserved for all later time steps up to a horizon T \(200 steps in the experiments\). A goal state is accepted only if the cell is not reserved at that time or later by a higher-priority robot, because the robot must be able to remain there.)
/E (For a fleet, planning is performed over states \(x, y, t\). From \(x, y, t\) a robot may move to a neighbouring free cell or wait in place, arriving at time t + 1; every action costs one time step. The heuristic is the Manhattan distance to the goal, which remains admissible in the time dimension. Robots are planned in a fixed priority order. After a robot is planned, its path is written to the reservation table \(Section 4.7\). A robot that reaches its goal stays there, so its goal cell is reserved for all later time steps up to a horizon T \(200 steps in the experiments\). A goal state is accepted only if the cell is not reserved at that time or later by a higher-priority robot, because the robot must be able to remain there.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
