/Title (Greedy Best-First: key = h\(n\) only; a node is assigned its parent on first discovery and never re-opened.)
/Dest [35 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 5141 0 R
/Prev 5185 0 R
/Next 5160 0 R
>>
endobj
5174 0 obj
<<
/Title (For a fleet, planning is performed over states \(x, y, t\). From \(x, y, t\) a robot may move to a neighbouring free cell or wait in place, arriving at time t + 1; every action costs one time step. The heuristic is the Manhattan distance to the goal, which remains admissible in the time dimension. Robots are planned in a fixed priority order. After a robot is planned, its path is written to the reservation table \(Section 4.7\). A robot that reaches its goal stays there, so its goal cell is reserved for all later time steps up to a horizon T \(200 steps in the experiments\). A goal state is accepted only if the cell is not reserved at that time or later by a higher-priority robot, because the robot must be able to remain there.)
/Dest [35 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 5162 0 R
>>
endobj
5175 0 obj
<<
/Title (Vertex conflict: two robots occupy the same cell at the same time step. Prevented by a reserved set of \(cell, time\) pairs.)
/Dest [35 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 5151 0 R
/Prev 5163 0 R
/Next 5176 0 R
>>
endobj
5176 0 obj
<<
